---
term: Charter model
aka:
  - Standardized model
section: building-blocks
order: 25
definition: The standard description of any charter by its dimensions, parameters, states, transitions, and outputs.
seeAlso:
  - charter
  - meeting-of-interests
  - centralization
---
The framework's job is to make every charter a known problem. The charter model does that by describing every charter in the same terms, so that heuristics from well-studied theories apply to it. The framework delineates the model, and each charter's design sets its values.

The model has the following parts:

| Part | Contents |
|---|---|
| Dimensions | [[Commons]], [[jurisdiction]], membership, articles, and delegated functions |
| Parameters | Settings that the articles control, such as membership tiers, [[centralization]], amendment thresholds, and exit terms |
| States | The charter's stage in its life cycle, and each party's [[status]] and tier on each commons |
| Transitions | [[Admission]], [[associative consolidation]], [[commission]], [[conversion]], [[exit]], [[secession]], [[escalation]], and [[dissolution]] |
| Outputs | Decisions, executions, and measurements |

Each part maps to an existing theory. Mechanism design applies to decisions, principal-agent theory to delegated functions, transaction cost economics to consolidation, fiscal federalism to centralization, and Elinor Ostrom's work on the commons to jurisdiction.

The model is a first version and is expected to grow.
